# ── Byte-exact engine assets ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Committed .inf_* payloads are locked byte-for-byte by tests (generator locks,
# forever-load schema fixtures, golden scenes). Line-ending conversion on
# checkout would silently break those locks on Windows (it did: Coyote.inf_act
# is JSON text, and autocrlf checkout CRLF'd it under the Windows CI runner).
# Never let git touch them.
*.inf_lvl -text
*.inf_act -text
*.inf_fn -text
*.inf_mat -text
*.inf_mati -text
*.inf_tex -text
*.inf_pcg -text
*.inf_enum -text
*.inf_struct -text
*.inf_table -text
*.inf_anim -text
*.inf_skel -text
*.inf_sm -text
# P29.6: the `.inf_sm` TEXT face (pillar S1). It is a sidecar rather than a
# payload, so nothing locks its bytes yet -- but `phase29_gate` reads it, edits
# one line of it and compares the result, and the whole point of the file is
# that a diff of it is reviewable. A checkout that CRLF'd it would make the
# committed line endings a property of the developer's platform, which is the
# exact defect the block above records paying for once already.
*.inf_sm.txt -text
# .inf_biomes (P19.2): a BiomeSet is deterministic bincode whose bytes a cook
# validates and a fixture will lock; never let git rewrite them.
*.inf_biomes -text
# .inf_pack: the frozen v1 pack fixture (P16.1 back-compat gate) is compared
# byte-for-byte; a cooked ship pack is binary by construction.
*.inf_pack -text
*.png -text

# TOML (asset sidecars included) participates in byte-determinism tests and is
# always emitted with LF; check it out that way everywhere.
*.toml text eol=lf

# Rust sources are READ BY TESTS, not only compiled (P22.4).
#
# The mirror gates (`projector_mirror`, `viewport_pump_mirror`, `grammar_span_mirror`)
# and the P22.4 trig-law gate all `include_str!`/`read_to_string` a `.rs` file and
# index into it — `find("\n}\n")`, `rfind('\n')`, line walks. With `core.autocrlf =
# true` and no attribute, every one of those files is checked out CRLF on Windows,
# where `"\n}\n"` does not occur and the search returns `None`. The mirrors defend
# themselves with `.replace("\r\n", "\n")` in twenty places; relying on every future
# gate to remember is the same bet this file's first paragraph already lost once.
#
# `text eol=lf` does not change what is stored (git normalizes to LF on check-IN
# regardless) — it fixes what is written to the working tree, so a Windows checkout
# and a Linux one hand a test the same bytes.
*.rs text eol=lf

# WGSL is read by tests for the same reason (P25.2). `inf-photo-gpu`'s parity
# gate strips comment lines out of every shader in its `SHADERS` table and then
# asserts what the CODE contains — that it never calls `fma()` (which would
# guarantee the contraction the CPU reference does not perform) and never calls
# `round()` (round-half-to-even in WGSL against Rust's round-half-away-from-zero).
# `str::lines()` happens to tolerate a trailing `\r`, so today the check survives
# a CRLF checkout by luck rather than by rule. The rule is cheaper than the luck.
*.wgsl text eol=lf

# Markdown is READ BY TESTS too (P29.6). `committed_sample_matches_generators`
# byte-pins every file of `samples/phase29-locomotion`, including its README —
# the first .md whose bytes a gate compares against a generator's LF output. On
# the Windows CI runner an autocrlf checkout handed that pin CRLF and the gate
# red was a property of the platform, not the content (the block above's story,
# fourth telling). Stored blobs are already LF; this fixes what checkout writes.
*.md text eol=lf

# TypeScript is GENERATED by ts-rs, which writes LF (island phase I1; the
# certification's D-26). Without this, every Windows contributor finished a green
# battery with ~150 dirty `editor/studio/src/bindings/*.ts` files whose
# `git diff --numstat` reports **zero changed lines** — pure checkout churn, and
# a drift-check job that has to be told to ignore it. Same fix, same reason, as
# the `.rs` / `.wgsl` / `.md` blocks above: this does not change what is stored
# (git normalizes to LF on check-IN regardless), it fixes what checkout writes.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
